{#
   THE ADMIN LIST PAGE — one page shape for every admin list.

   `_catalogue.html.twig` did this for the public side and `_data_table.html.twig`
   did it for the table itself. This is the layer between them: the page *around*
   the table, which was still being written out by hand fifteen times.

   ⚠️ **What was actually wrong.** There were two skeletons in the codebase and
   nothing said which was current. Eleven pages used `admin-page` + `admin-layout`;
   five used `main-content` + `admin-main` + `admin-content-grid` +
   `admin-main-content` — four nested wrappers to do the job of one. Same job,
   same sidebar, different DOM, so they could never be restyled together.

   ⚠️ **The flashes were the tell.** This shell renders them; before it, one page
   styled its own success flash with a `style=""` attribute carrying a hardcoded
   green. A page that has to remember to draw its own confirmation message is a
   page that will one day forget.

   Use it with `{% embed %}`:

     {% embed 'site/_admin_list.html.twig' with {
         title: 'admin_places.title'|trans,
         description: 'admin_places.list_description'|trans,
         action_label: 'admin_places.list_action'|trans,
         action_route: 'app_admin_place_new',
         panel_title: 'admin_places.list_panel'|trans,
     } %}
         {% block content %}…the table…{% endblock %}
     {% endembed %}

   Parameters, all optional except `title`:

     title          the page heading
     description    the line under it
     action_label   the "add" button's words. Omitted = no button.
     action_route   where it goes
     action_params  route parameters, if it needs any
     panel_title    the heading on the panel that holds the table
     panel_note     a quiet line on the right of that heading
     sidebar_variant  passed through to `_admin_sidebar` (default: standard)

   Blocks:

     filters      a `.filters-bar` form, above the panel. Omit for no filters.
     content      the table — normally an `{% embed %}` of `_data_table`
     panel_extra  anything after the table, inside the panel
     after        anything after the panel entirely

   ⚠️ This template computes NOTHING and knows what none of these things are.
   Same rule as `_catalogue`: an `{% if %}` on entity type here and it has
   stopped being a shell.
#}
{% include 'site/_header.html.twig' %}

<main class="admin-page">
    <header class="admin-page-header">
        <div class="admin-page-header-inner">
            <div>
                <h1>{{ title }}</h1>
                {% if description|default('') %}<p>{{ description }}</p>{% endif %}
            </div>
            {# The one add-button shape (S82). ⚠️ Not `btn btn-primary` — that is
               the PUBLIC button and renders at roughly twice the size here, which
               is how the same action came to be a slab on one page and a chip on
               the next. #}
            {% if action_label|default('') and action_route|default('') %}
                <a class="btn-link btn-primary-admin" href="{{ path(action_route, action_params|default({})) }}">{{ action_label }}</a>
            {% endif %}
            {% block header_extra %}{% endblock %}
        </div>
    </header>

    <div class="admin-layout"{% if list_client_filter|default(false) %} data-controller="admin-list-filter"{% endif %}>
        {% include 'site/_admin_sidebar.html.twig' with {
            admin_sidebar_variant: sidebar_variant|default('standard'),
        } %}

        {# ⚠️ ONE grid item, holding both panels. `.admin-layout` is
           `260px 1fr`, so filters and table as separate children would land in
           different columns — the table would wrap back under the sidebar. #}
        <div class="admin-column">
        {# ⚠️ Filters get their own panel ABOVE the table, not a row inside it.
           That is what the five pages that had filters were already doing, and
           it is the better shape: the controls that change the list are visibly
           not part of the list. `has_filters` rather than testing the block,
           because Twig cannot ask whether a block is empty.

           ⚠️ **The sub-venue picker moved INSIDE this panel in S130c.** It used to
           sit above it in a full-width bar of its own, which put the one filter
           that scopes every other filter outside the box holding them. It is the
           first group in the panel now — read top to bottom, the list narrows
           from where, to what kind, to which words. #}
        {% set show_venue_picker = venue_context|default(null) and venue_context.venues|length > 1 %}
        {% set show_tools = list_tools|default(false) %}
        {% if show_venue_picker or show_tools %}
            {% set list_query = list_query|default({}) %}
            <div class="admin-list-tools">
                {% if show_venue_picker %}
                    {% include 'site/_venue_context.html.twig' with {venue_context: venue_context, list_route: list_route, list_route_params: list_route_params|default({}), list_query: list_query} only %}
                {% endif %}
                {% if show_tools %}
                {% if list_tiles|default([]) is not empty %}
                    <nav class="ml-cats" aria-label="{{ list_tiles_label|default(title) }}">
                        {% for tile in list_tiles %}
                            <a class="ml-tile{{ tile.active|default(false) ? ' is-on' : '' }}" href="{{ path(list_route, list_tiles_preserve_query|default(true) ? list_query|merge(tile.query|default({})) : tile.query|default({})) }}"{% if list_client_filter|default(false) %} data-admin-list-filter-tile data-admin-list-filter-category="{{ tile.category|default('all') }}" data-action="click->admin-list-filter#category"{% endif %}>
                                {% if tile.icon_slug|default('') %}<span class="ml-tile-icon">{% include 'site/_category_icon.html.twig' with { slug: tile.icon_slug } only %}</span>{% endif %}
                                <span class="ml-tile-label">{{ tile.label_is_key|default(false) ? tile.label|trans : tile.label }}</span><span class="ml-tile-count">{{ tile.count }}</span>
                            </a>
                        {% endfor %}
                    </nav>
                {% endif %}
                {% for group in list_tile_groups|default([]) %}
                    <span class="ml-filter-group-label">{{ group.label }}</span>
                    <nav class="ml-cats" aria-label="{{ group.label }}">
                        {% for tile in group.tiles %}
                            {# ⚠️ `label_is_key` is honoured here too (S133). The single-row
                               path above has read the flag since it was introduced; this
                               grouped path never did, so `/admin/machines` printed the
                               literal `admin_list.all` as its "All" tile — the controller
                               was setting the flag correctly and only one of the two
                               renderers was listening. #}
                            <a class="ml-tile{{ tile.active|default(false) ? ' is-on' : '' }}" href="{{ path(list_route, group.preserve_query|default(true) ? list_query|merge(tile.query|default({})) : tile.query|default({})) }}">
                                <span class="ml-tile-label">{{ tile.label_is_key|default(false) ? tile.label|trans : tile.label }}</span><span class="ml-tile-count">{{ tile.count }}</span>
                            </a>
                        {% endfor %}
                    </nav>
                {% endfor %}
                <form class="ml-filters" method="get" action="{{ path(list_route) }}"{% if list_client_filter|default(false) %} data-action="submit->admin-list-filter#search"{% endif %}>
                    {% for key, value in list_query %}<input type="hidden" name="{{ key }}" value="{{ value }}">{% endfor %}
                    <div class="ml-field">
                        <label for="admin-list-q">{{ 'common.search'|trans }}</label>
                        <input id="admin-list-q" type="search" name="q" value="{{ list_search|default('') }}" placeholder="{{ list_search_placeholder|default('') }}"{% if list_client_filter|default(false) %} data-admin-list-filter-search data-action="input->admin-list-filter#filter"{% endif %}>
                    </div>
                    <button class="ml-btn" type="submit">{{ 'common.search'|trans }}</button>
                    {% if list_search|default('') or list_query %}<a class="ml-reset" href="{{ path(list_route) }}">{{ 'common.reset'|trans }}</a>{% endif %}
                </form>
                {% if has_advanced_filters|default(false) %}
                    <details class="workspace-advanced-filters">
                        <summary>{{ 'workspace.more_filters'|trans }}</summary>
                        <div class="workspace-advanced-filters-body">{% block advanced_filters %}{% endblock %}</div>
                    </details>
                {% endif %}
                {% endif %}
            </div>
        {% endif %}
        {# ⚠️ Unchanged condition, decoupled from the panel above. The two used to
           be one `if/elseif` on `list_tools`; now that the sub-venue picker can
           open that panel on its own, a page with `has_filters` and no
           `list_tools` would have lost its filters the moment a second sub-venue
           existed — a bug that appears on the operator's install and on no
           developer's. #}
        {% if not show_tools and has_filters|default(false) %}
            <div class="admin-panel admin-panel-filters">
                {% block filters %}{% endblock %}
            </div>
        {% endif %}

        {% set current_filter_chips = list_active_filters|default(workspace_filter_chips(app.request.query.all)) %}
        {% if current_filter_chips is not empty %}
            <div class="workspace-filter-chips" aria-label="{{ 'workspace.active_filters'|trans }}">
                <span class="workspace-filter-chips-label">{{ 'workspace.active_filters'|trans }}</span>
                {% for chip in current_filter_chips %}
                    <a class="workspace-filter-chip" href="{{ path(list_route, chip.remove_query|default({})) }}">
                        <span>{{ chip.label|default(chip.label_key|trans) }} : {{ chip.value|default('') }}</span><span aria-hidden="true">×</span><span class="sr-only">{{ 'workspace.remove_filter'|trans }}</span>
                    </a>
                {% endfor %}
                <a class="workspace-filter-reset" href="{{ path(list_route) }}">{{ 'workspace.clear_filters'|trans }}</a>
            </div>
        {% endif %}

        <section class="admin-panel">
            <div class="admin-panel-header">
                <h2>{{ panel_title|default(title) }}</h2>
                {% if panel_note|default('') %}<span class="admin-panel-note">{{ panel_note }}</span>{% endif %}
                {% block panel_header_extra %}{% endblock %}
            </div>

            {# ⚠️ Emitted here, for every list, rather than left to the caller.
               `admin-flash` already carries the tone colours; a page writing its
               own is how one of them ended up with an inline hardcoded green. #}
            {% for message in app.flashes('success') %}<div class="admin-flash success">{{ message }}</div>{% endfor %}
            {% for message in app.flashes('error') %}<div class="admin-flash error">{{ message }}</div>{% endfor %}

            {% block content %}{% endblock %}
            {% block panel_extra %}{% endblock %}
        </section>
        </div>
    </div>

    {% block after %}{% endblock %}
</main>

{# ⚠️ **The footer belongs to the shell, not to the caller.** S129 established
   that here, for the 25 pages on this list shell. S130b found the same split one
   level up — `base.html.twig` had no footer at all, so the 41 pages that do not
   come through this file ended in whitespace — and moved the include there, which
   is the only place that covers every page. This file no longer emits one;
   adding it back would render it twice. #}
